WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.140
Reallocation and resizing deserve special attention because they combine lifetime, ownership, capacity, and pointer validity.

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.960
A buffer may begin as one allocation and later grow or shrink.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:25.100
If code keeps interior pointers, cached base pointers, or borrowed views into that storage, resizing can invalidate assumptions that were true a moment earlier.

4
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.100
The realloc function may move an allocation.

5
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.240
When it moves, older references to the previous storage are no longer valid.

6
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:39.500
That includes aliases to the base pointer and pointers into the middle of the old buffer.

7
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.480
Code that resizes a buffer should review who else can still reach the old storage.

8
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.080
Assigning realloc directly to the original pointer can lose the allocation on failure.

9
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:54.860
If the resize fails, the original allocation remains valid and still owned by the caller.

10
00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:01.840
A temporary pointer pattern preserves the original pointer until the new allocation is known to have succeeded.

11
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.480
Capacity and count must be updated only when the resize operation succeeds.

12
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.700
If a grow operation fails, the buffer should still describe the old valid allocation.

13
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:21.200
Updating metadata too early can leave the object claiming capacity it does not have, or losing track of data it still owns.

14
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.280
Other aliases into the old allocation must be considered after resizing.

15
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:35.900
If a structure stores both a base pointer and a cursor pointer into the same buffer, a successful move requires updating dependent pointers or recomputing them from offsets.

16
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.400
Storing offsets instead of interior pointers can sometimes make resizing safer.

17
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:47.320
Resize logic should keep the object in a valid state whether the operation succeeds or fails.

18
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:54.460
The caller should know whether the buffer changed, whether old data remains available, and whether any borrowed views must be considered invalid.

19
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.920
A clear resize contract prevents lifetime confusion from spreading beyond the resize function.
